WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.285
Lesson 5.8.

00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:06.145
Reading smart-contract code: enough to verify claims.

00:00:06.145 --> 00:00:08.562
By the end, you'll be able to check what

00:00:08.562 --> 00:00:12.100
a protocol actually says about itself, directly against its

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.225
own verified code, without needing to be a developer.

00:00:16.525 --> 00:00:17.815
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.815 --> 00:00:19.872
Access control, the actual words to

00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:22.000
search for, and why they matter.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.251
Dangerous powers, the specific functions worth finding

00:00:25.251 --> 00:00:27.325
before you ever trust a claim.

00:00:27.325 --> 00:00:31.847
Upgradeability, and external-call ordering, the reentrancy pattern from

00:00:31.847 --> 00:00:35.225
Lesson five point five, spotted directly in code.

00:00:35.225 --> 00:00:37.771
And a full worked example, checking a real

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:40.435
fixed-supply claim against its own real code.

00:00:41.735 --> 00:00:42.800
Access control.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.154
Here's exactly what to search for, directly in a

00:00:47.154 --> 00:00:51.255
contract's own verified Solidity code, on any block explorer.

00:00:51.255 --> 00:00:54.117
The words onlyOwner, onlyRole, followed by

00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:57.080
a specific role name, or onlyGovernance.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.306
Every one of these guards a specific

00:00:59.306 --> 00:01:02.194
function, meaning only whoever holds that exact

00:01:02.194 --> 00:01:04.058
role can actually call it.

00:01:04.058 --> 00:01:06.461
Search the code for these words directly,

00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:08.978
and you've found exactly who controls what,

00:01:08.978 --> 00:01:11.038
without reading a single other line.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:14.952
Worth being direct about why this single search

00:01:14.952 --> 00:01:17.512
is genuinely worth doing, even if you've never

00:01:17.512 --> 00:01:19.773
written a line of code in your life.

00:01:19.773 --> 00:01:23.061
These three words are simply text, searchable exactly

00:01:23.061 --> 00:01:25.188
like any other word on a page.

00:01:25.188 --> 00:01:29.090
Finding them tells you precisely which functions are restricted,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.731
and to whom, which is most of what actually

00:01:31.731 --> 00:01:34.613
matters about a contract's real power structure.

00:01:35.913 --> 00:01:38.609
Here's exactly how to actually run this search

00:01:38.609 --> 00:01:41.778
yourself, on any block explorer's own code viewer.

00:01:41.778 --> 00:01:45.078
Open the contract's verified code tab directly.

00:01:45.078 --> 00:01:48.302
Use your browser's own find function, Control F,

00:01:48.302 --> 00:01:51.103
or Command F, to search the full page.

00:01:51.103 --> 00:01:55.457
Search specifically for onlyOwner, onlyRole, and onlyGovernance,

00:01:55.457 --> 00:01:57.467
one at a time, and note every

00:01:57.467 --> 00:01:59.610
single function each one guards.

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.000
Quick check.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.745
What does onlyOwner on a mint function actually mean?

00:02:09.165 --> 00:02:11.723
The answer: the owner can mint new tokens

00:02:11.723 --> 00:02:15.195
whenever they genuinely like, with nothing else required.

00:02:16.495 --> 00:02:18.385
Dangerous powers to search for.

00:02:19.685 --> 00:02:22.830
Here's exactly what each of these actually lets someone

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.770
do, once you've found which role controls them.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.275
Mint creates new tokens, out of nothing.

00:02:28.275 --> 00:02:31.545
Pause can freeze the entire contract, instantly.

00:02:31.545 --> 00:02:34.339
UpgradeTo can swap the underlying logic, the

00:02:34.339 --> 00:02:36.875
pattern from Lesson five point four.

00:02:36.875 --> 00:02:39.848
SetOracle and setFee can change what price

00:02:39.848 --> 00:02:42.567
or cost the contract actually uses.

00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:45.537
And withdraw, sweep, or rescue functions can

00:02:45.537 --> 00:02:48.309
move user funds directly, sometimes for a

00:02:48.309 --> 00:02:51.147
genuinely legitimate reason, sometimes not.

00:02:52.447 --> 00:02:55.727
Worth being fair about that last category specifically,

00:02:55.727 --> 00:02:58.420
since the names alone can sound alarming even

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.180
when the function is genuinely legitimate.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.796
A rescue function recovering accidentally-sent tokens

00:03:04.796 --> 00:03:07.640
is a real, common, reasonable feature.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.249
The actual question is never simply whether

00:03:10.249 --> 00:03:12.799
the function exists; it's who controls it,

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:15.763
and whether it's restricted specifically to funds

00:03:15.763 --> 00:03:18.135
that genuinely don't belong to any user.

00:03:19.435 --> 00:03:23.368
Here's what actually separates a well-scoped rescue function from

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:26.290
a dangerously broad one, in the code itself.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:29.979
A well-scoped one explicitly checks the token isn't the

00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:34.392
contract's own primary asset, restricting it specifically to funds

00:03:34.392 --> 00:03:37.590
that genuinely aren't a user's real deposit.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.466
A broad one simply lets the owner withdraw any

00:03:40.466 --> 00:03:44.138
token, or even the contract's own native balance, directly,

00:03:44.138 --> 00:03:46.770
with no such restriction written in at all.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.105
Upgradeability, and call ordering.

00:03:51.405 --> 00:03:53.384
Here's exactly what to search for to

00:03:53.384 --> 00:03:55.202
spot a proxy directly, and who to

00:03:55.202 --> 00:03:57.000
check once you've found one.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.622
The words delegatecall, or implementation, or upgradeTo,

00:04:00.622 --> 00:04:03.228
appearing anywhere in the code, are your

00:04:03.228 --> 00:04:05.625
signal it's an upgradeable proxy.

00:04:05.625 --> 00:04:08.226
Once you've spotted it, go find the actual

00:04:08.226 --> 00:04:11.129
admin, exactly the check covered back in Lesson

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:14.334
five point four, since that's who genuinely controls

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:16.330
what this contract does tomorrow.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.896
Worth being precise about the classic reentrancy shape specifically,

00:04:21.896 --> 00:04:26.030
since it's genuinely recognisable even without deep expertise.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.251
Watch for a contract calling out to

00:04:28.251 --> 00:04:31.830
another, external contract, before it's actually finished

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.105
updating its own internal state.

00:04:34.105 --> 00:04:37.909
That specific ordering, external call first, state update

00:04:37.909 --> 00:04:41.384
second, is exactly the pattern behind the reentrancy

00:04:41.384 --> 00:04:44.270
risk covered back in Lesson five point five.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.660
Quick check.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.990
What code pattern specifically suggests a reentrancy risk?

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.251
The answer: an external call made before the

00:04:57.251 --> 00:05:00.975
contract actually finishes updating its own internal state.

00:05:02.275 --> 00:05:04.220
Unlimited parameters, and events.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.005
Here's the actual difference a limit makes, made concrete.

00:05:09.005 --> 00:05:11.245
A fee parameter with a genuine hard

00:05:11.245 --> 00:05:13.423
cap, say a maximum of one percent,

00:05:13.423 --> 00:05:16.036
written directly into the code, can never

00:05:16.036 --> 00:05:19.012
exceed that, regardless of who controls it.

00:05:19.012 --> 00:05:21.553
A fee parameter with no such limit at all

00:05:21.553 --> 00:05:24.940
can technically be set to one hundred percent, entirely

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.602
at whoever's own discretion holds that role.

00:05:28.902 --> 00:05:32.917
Here's that same capped-versus-uncapped difference, expressed as

00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:35.943
actual numbers, purely illustrative, to make the

00:05:35.943 --> 00:05:38.837
real stakes concrete rather than abstract.

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:41.632
A genuinely capped fee parameter might allow

00:05:41.632 --> 00:05:44.303
a maximum of one percent, written directly

00:05:44.303 --> 00:05:46.467
into the code as a hard limit.

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:49.066
An uncapped one has no such ceiling at all,

00:05:49.066 --> 00:05:51.843
meaning it could technically be set as high as

00:05:51.843 --> 00:05:55.742
one hundred percent, entirely at the role-holder's own discretion.

00:05:57.042 --> 00:06:00.704
Worth naming events specifically too, since they're genuinely

00:06:00.704 --> 00:06:03.657
useful for exactly the kind of ongoing monitoring

00:06:03.657 --> 00:06:06.497
this whole module has been building toward.

00:06:06.497 --> 00:06:08.853
What a contract actually logs, as an

00:06:08.853 --> 00:06:11.274
event, is what you, or any monitoring

00:06:11.274 --> 00:06:14.121
tool, can actually watch after the fact.

00:06:14.121 --> 00:06:17.602
A contract logging every single admin action gives

00:06:17.602 --> 00:06:21.083
you real, ongoing visibility; one that logs almost

00:06:21.083 --> 00:06:24.291
nothing leaves you flying blind between checks.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.680
Quick check.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.388
Why does it actually matter to

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:30.095
check a parameter's own limits?

00:06:34.515 --> 00:06:37.344
The answer: an unlimited setter, like a fee

00:06:37.344 --> 00:06:40.430
or an oracle address, can be changed instantly,

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.645
to genuinely harm users, with nothing stopping it.

00:06:44.945 --> 00:06:45.950
Worked example.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:50.179
Here's the actual line of code itself, exactly

00:06:50.179 --> 00:06:53.234
as the source material presents it, worth seeing

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:55.715
in full rather than just described.

00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:58.777
Function mint, taking an address and an amount,

00:06:58.777 --> 00:07:02.093
marked external, guarded by onlyOwner, and its body

00:07:02.093 --> 00:07:06.220
simply calls the internal underscore-mint function directly.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:07.967
Four words matter most here:

00:07:07.967 --> 00:07:10.195
external, onlyOwner, and mint itself.

00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:13.548
Here's the claim, and exactly what

00:07:13.548 --> 00:07:15.490
its own code actually shows.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.190
A token's own marketing states plainly: fixed supply.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.167
Its verified code, on the explorer, contains

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:25.840
a mint function, guarded specifically by onlyOwner.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:28.861
That single guarded function means the owner can

00:07:28.861 --> 00:07:32.930
genuinely create unlimited new tokens, entirely contradicting the

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:37.060
fixed-supply claim, unless something else specifically prevents it.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.879
Worth being precise about the specific two conditions that

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:45.636
would actually make this fixed-supply claim true, despite that

00:07:45.636 --> 00:07:48.620
mint function genuinely existing in the code.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:52.405
Either ownership has been fully renounced, meaning no address

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:55.702
controls it at all anymore, or it's held specifically

00:07:55.702 --> 00:07:59.670
by a timelocked governance contract, giving real, public warning

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.295
before any mint could ever actually happen.

00:08:03.595 --> 00:08:06.566
Here's exactly how to actually check which of those

00:08:06.566 --> 00:08:09.780
two is true, directly on the block explorer itself.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:11.965
Go to the contract's own Read tab.

00:08:11.965 --> 00:08:14.380
Find the specific function called owner.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:15.915
Call it directly, and read

00:08:15.915 --> 00:08:17.920
the actual address it returns.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.917
If that address is the well-known zero address,

00:08:20.917 --> 00:08:24.351
ownership is genuinely renounced; if it's a timelocked

00:08:24.351 --> 00:08:28.035
governance contract, check that timelock's own real length.

00:08:29.335 --> 00:08:30.795
Here's your checklist.

00:08:30.795 --> 00:08:32.445
Do it for any contract whose

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.680
claims you actually want to trust.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:38.275
Search the code for every owner or role-guarded function.

00:08:38.275 --> 00:08:41.043
List every single one that can mint, pause,

00:08:41.043 --> 00:08:44.299
upgrade, change fees or oracles, or move funds.

00:08:44.299 --> 00:08:47.625
And check exactly who holds those specific roles, on

00:08:47.625 --> 00:08:50.574
the Read tab, and behind what timelock, if any.

00:08:51.874 --> 00:08:53.119
Let's recap.

00:08:53.119 --> 00:08:56.643
Search verified code for onlyOwner, onlyRole, and

00:08:56.643 --> 00:08:59.904
onlyGovernance, to find who controls what.

00:08:59.904 --> 00:09:03.534
Find every mint, pause, upgradeTo, setFee, setOracle,

00:09:03.534 --> 00:09:06.859
and withdraw or rescue function specifically.

00:09:06.859 --> 00:09:08.721
Watch for an external call made

00:09:08.721 --> 00:09:11.524
before state updates, the reentrancy shape.

00:09:11.524 --> 00:09:14.384
Check whether parameters are genuinely capped.

00:09:14.384 --> 00:09:17.705
And always verify a claim directly against the owner,

00:09:17.705 --> 00:09:20.719
on the Read tab, not against the marketing alone.

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:24.614
Worth closing Module five on the actual

00:09:24.614 --> 00:09:27.403
point this entire module has been building

00:09:27.403 --> 00:09:29.844
toward, across all eight lessons.

00:09:29.844 --> 00:09:32.973
Every dependency, the chain, the bridge, the L2,

00:09:32.973 --> 00:09:35.719
the oracle, the contract, and now the code

00:09:35.719 --> 00:09:39.423
itself, is genuinely checkable, by you, directly, without

00:09:39.423 --> 00:09:41.594
needing anyone else's word for it.

00:09:42.894 --> 00:09:45.899
Search for the guard words, list every dangerous

00:09:45.899 --> 00:09:49.449
function, and verify claims against owner() directly.

00:09:49.449 --> 00:09:51.444
That's the end of Module 5.

00:09:51.444 --> 00:09:52.899
Next up, Module 6.
